 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "EXETER. The Quay, the Cathedral and a cream tea, north. The Exe Estuary and Dartmoor, south. Cream first."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "EXETER CATHEDRAL. Begun by the Normans, whose two great square towers still stand, then rebuilt in the Gothic style from about 1270. Inside, its stone vault runs unbroken from end to end for about ninety-six metres, the longest stretch of medieval Gothic vaulting in the world.",
   "plaque": "devon_exeter_cathedral",
   "title": "Exeter Cathedral"
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "THE EXETER BOOK. Given to the cathedral by Leofric, its first bishop, who died in 1072. It is the largest collection of Old English poetry anyone has, and it is full of riddles: nearly a hundred of them, some holy, some rude, all very old.",
   "plaque": "devon_exeter_book",
   "people": "anglo_saxons",
   "title": "The Exeter Book",
   "theme": "arts"
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE CITY WALL. The Romans built it around AD 200 to protect Isca Dumnoniorum, the town that became Exeter. Saxons, Normans and Tudors patched it in their turn, and most of the circuit still stands.",
   "plaque": "devon_city_wall",
   "title": "Exeter's City Wall"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "ROUGEMONT CASTLE. William the Conqueror besieged Exeter in 1068, then built a castle on its red volcanic hill: rouge mont. Its gatehouse is one of the oldest Norman buildings in Britain.",
   "plaque": "devon_rougemont",
   "title": "Rougemont Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "THE UNDERGROUND PASSAGES. In the 1300s the city dug vaulted tunnels to carry lead pipes of fresh spring water in from outside the walls, so they could be mended without digging up the street. Nowhere else in Britain can you walk through anything quite like them.",
   "plaque": "devon_underground_passages",
   "title": "The Underground Passages"
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE HOUSE THAT MOVED. In 1961 this Tudor merchant's house stood in the way of a new road, so it was jacked up, put on wheels and moved down the street. It is still there. So is the road.",
   "plaque": "devon_house_that_moved",
   "title": "The House That Moved"
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "NORTHERNHAY GARDENS. Laid out in 1612 along the old wall, and said to be the oldest public open space in England. People have been sitting on benches here for over four hundred years.",
   "plaque": "devon_northernhay",
   "title": "Northernhay Gardens"
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "THE CUSTOM HOUSE. Built in 1680 and 1681, it is the oldest substantial brick building in Exeter: the merchants of the wool trade had money to show off. Cloth from the Devon mills went out from this quay all over Europe.",
   "plaque": "devon_custom_house",
   "title": "The Custom House"
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "THE EXETER SHIP CANAL. In the 1200s a weir was built across the Exe and boats could no longer reach the city. So in the 1560s Exeter dug a canal round it, one of the oldest artificial waterways in England.",
   "plaque": "devon_exeter_canal",
   "title": "The Exeter Ship Canal"
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE GUILDHALL. Exeter's council has met on this spot since at least the 1100s, which makes the Guildhall one of the oldest civic buildings in England still in use. The pillared front was added in Tudor times.",
   "plaque": "devon_guildhall",
   "title": "The Guildhall"
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "devon_landsky_met_office",
   "title": "The Met Office",
   "text": "The Met Office began in 1854 as a small department of the Board of Trade under Robert FitzRoy, who had captained HMS Beagle with Darwin aboard. In 1861 he sent the first storm warnings to the ports by telegraph and printed the first public forecasts, a word he coined. Its headquarters are here in Exeter, where a supercomputer now does the sums FitzRoy did with a barometer and a pencil."
  },
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "devon_landsky_changeable",
   "title": "Why the weather keeps changing",
   "text": "Britain sits where air masses meet: mild wet air off the Atlantic, cold air down from the Arctic, dry air from the continent, hot air up from Africa. Overhead runs the jet stream, a river of wind miles up that steers Atlantic lows at these isles one after another. When the jet sits north of us the weather is warm and dry; south of us, cool and wet. It wobbles, so we get four seasons in a day."
  }
 ],
